L07/L08 cards: 47
SC1-c134 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: What is the running maximum M_H of B on [0,H]?
  B: M_H= max_(0≤ s≤ H)B_s. The maximum exists because the path is continuous on the compact interval [0,H].
SC1-c135 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Definition 1.1 of Lesson 7 and B_0 = 0 (Definition 1.1 of Lesson 1)
  F: For standard Brownian motion with B_0=0 and continuous paths, is the running maximum M_H always nonnegative, and why?
  B: Yes, M_H≥ 0, because B_0=0 is one of the values in the maximum.
SC1-c136 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: At a fixed horizon H>0, which function of a describes the law of the running maximum M_H?
  B: Its tail function a↦P(M_H≥ a), for real a.
SC1-c137 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Theorem 1.3, Section 1, via Theorem 2.5 of Lesson 6, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Theorem 1.3 (tail of the running maximum). Hypotheses: B is a standard Brownian motion with continuous paths and B_0=0; Assumptions A3 and A4 of Lesson 5 hold; H>0 and a>0. What is P(M_H≥ a)?
  B: 2 (1-Φ(a/√H))=erfc(a/√(2H)), where Φ is the standard normal distribution function.
SC1-c138 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Remark 1.4, Section 1, assuming the hypotheses of Theorem 1.3
  F: Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, Remark 1.4: for fixed a>0, what does P(M_H≥ a) tend to as H↓ 0 and as H→∞?
  B: 0 as H↓ 0, and 1 as H→∞. The argument a/√H tends to ∞ as H↓ 0 and to 0 as H→∞, and Φ is continuous with Φ(0)=1/2.
SC1-c139 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Corollary 1.5(a), Section 1, from Theorem 1.3, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Corollary 1.5(a). Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, for m≥ 0, what is P(M_H≤ m)?
  B: 2 Φ(m/√H)-1, under the hypotheses of Theorem 1.3.
SC1-c140 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Corollary 1.5(b), Section 1, assuming A5 and Theorem 1.3 (which assumes A3 and A4 of Lesson 5)
  F: Corollary 1.5(b). Assuming A5 (the tail formula for a nonnegative variable) and A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, what is E[M_H]?
  B: √(2H/π). The substitution m=√H u reduces the integral to ∫_0^∞(1-Φ(u)) du.
SC1-c141 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem P2 and the proof of Corollary 1.5(b), Section 1
  F: What is ∫_0^∞(1-Φ(u)) du?
  B: 1/√(2π), equal to φ(0), where φ(u)=e^(-u^2/2)/√(2π) is the standard normal density. Integration by parts on [0,R] and the bound R(1-Φ(R))≤φ(R)→ 0 give it.
SC1-c142 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Definition 2.1. For H>0 and an integer N≥ 1, what is the discretely monitored maximum M^(N)_H on the equal grid with s_k=kH/N?
  B: M^(N)_H= max_(0≤ k≤ N)B_(s_k), with s_k=kH/N for k from 0 to N.
SC1-c143 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Proposition 2.2(a), Section 2
  F: Proposition 2.2(a). Assuming the path is continuous on [0,H], how does the grid maximum M^(N)_H compare with the running maximum M_H?
  B: M^(N)_H≤ M_H, because the grid times s_k are points of [0,H].
SC1-c144 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Proposition 2.2(b), Section 2
  F: Proposition 2.2(b), on a continuous path. If N ≥ 1 and N′ ≥ 1 are integers and N divides N′, how do M^(N)_H and M^(N′)_H compare?
  B: M^(N)_H≤ M^(N')_H, because every grid time of the N-step grid is a grid time of the N'-step grid.
SC1-c145 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Corollary 2.3, Section 2, with Proposition 2.2(a) and Theorem 1.3 (closed form assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5)
  F: Corollary 2.3, under H1 (continuous paths). For every real a and every grid, how does P(M^(N)_H≥ a) compare with P(M_H≥ a)?
  B: P(M^(N)_H≥ a)≤P(M_H≥ a), because {M^(N)_H≥ a}⊆{M_H≥ a}. For a>0 the right side is, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, the closed form of Theorem 1.3.
SC1-c146 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Remark 2.4, Section 2, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, Remark 2.4: for a single step N=1 on [0,H] and a>0, what is P(M^(1)_H≥ a) relative to P(M_H≥ a)?
  B: M^(1)_H= max(0,B_H), so P(M^(1)_H≥ a)=P(B_H≥ a), which is exactly half of P(M_H≥ a).
SC1-c148 [L07] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part A, with binomial arithmetic in Problem E2
  F: Lab 1, Part A: how many of the 2^(16) walks of length 16 have running maximum at least 4, and what is the exact tail?
  B: 21778 walks, so the tail is 0.332306. It is the sum 14893 + 6885 of binomial counts for endpoint at least 4 and endpoint above 4.
SC1-c149 [L07] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED, one seed, 400 paths)
  F: Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED, 400 seeded paths on [0,1], fine grid of 256 steps): what proportion of grid maxima reach level 1 at 4 and at 256 steps, and how many pathwise nesting violations occur?
  B: 0.2275 at 4 steps and 0.3025 at 256 steps, against the closed form 0.3173 (SE 0.0210 and 0.0230). The count of nesting violations is 0.
SC1-c150 [L07] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part C (SIMULATED, one seed, 400 paths)
  F: Lab 1, Part C (SIMULATED, one seed): what is the mean grid maximum at 256 steps, and how does it compare with √(2/π)?
  B: 0.7586, with standard error 0.0317, below √(2/π)=0.797885. The lab's gap is 1.2 standard errors, so this sample does not separate the discrete bias from sampling error.
SC1-c151 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Section 3: does the tail formula apply to the discontinuous process B̃ of Lesson 1, Section 4?
  B: Not asserted. Lemma 2.1 of Lesson 6 needs continuous paths, and no tail formula is asserted for B̃, even though it has the same one-time laws as B.
SC1-c152 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Section 3: is the closed form for P(M_H≥ a) proved unconditionally?
  B: No. Theorem 1.3 holds under Assumptions A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, which are assumed, not proved.
SC1-c153 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proposition 2.2(a), Corollary 2.3, Theorem 1.3 (closed form assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5)
  F: On continuous paths, misconception: a grid maximum can overstate the running maximum, so the grid tail sits above the closed form. Is this right?
  B: No. Proposition 2.2(a) gives M^(N)_H≤ M_H on every continuous path, so Corollary 2.3 bounds the grid tail above by the continuous tail, which for a>0 is, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, the closed form of Theorem 1.3.
SC1-c154 [L07] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Misconception: any process with normal one-time marginals has the closed-form tail of M_H. Is this right?
  B: No. The tail identity depends on the whole path. The process B̃ of Lesson 1, Section 4 has the same one-time laws as B, but its paths are discontinuous, and no tail formula is asserted for it. Lemma 2.1 of Lesson 6 needs continuous paths, and Theorem 1.3 needs Assumptions A3 and A4.
SC1-c155 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem M2, Theorem 1.3, assuming A3 and A4
  F: Problem M2. Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, does P(M_H≥ a) depend on (a,H) only through a/√H? Give the closed form.
  B: Yes. It is 2(1-Φ(a/√H)), a function of a/√H alone.
SC1-c156 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem R1, Definition 1.1, evenness of the normal density
  F: Problem R1. For a<0, why does the closed form 2(1-Φ(a/√H)) fail to be a probability, and what is P(M_H≥ a) for a≤ 0?
  B: For a<0 the expression exceeds 1, because Φ(a/√H)<1/2. For a≤ 0, P(M_H≥ a)=1, since M_H≥ 0≥ a.
SC1-c157 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem R2, Theorem 1.3, assuming A3 and A4
  F: Problem R2. For fixed H>0 and a>0, what is P(M_H=a), assuming A3 and A4?
  B: 0. The events {M_H≥ a+1/n} increase to {M_H>a}, so continuity of probability from below and Theorem 1.3 give P(M_H>a)=P(M_H≥ a), so P(M_H=a)=0.
SC1-c159 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem E3, algebra of the sample variance of indicators
  F: Problem E3. For k successes in n indicators, what is the standard error of the sample proportion with divisor n-1?
  B: √(k(n-k)/(n^2(n-1))). For k=121 and n=400 this is 0.0230.
SC1-c161 [L08] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Definition 1.1. For a>0, what is the first-passage time τ_a of B at a?
  B: τ_a= inf{t≥ 0: B_t=a}, with inf ∅=∞.
SC1-c162 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Proposition 1.2, Section 1
  F: Proposition 1.2. Hypotheses: for a fixed outcome, s↦ B_s is continuous and B_0=0; a>0 and H≥ 0 are fixed. On the event {τ_a≤ H}, what is the value of B at τ_a?
  B: B_(τ_a)=a. On {τ_a≤ H}, τ_a is finite and is the infimum of the closed set {s: B_s=a}, so it belongs to that set.
SC1-c164 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Corollary 1.4, Section 1, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Corollary 1.4. Under the hypotheses of Proposition 1.2, and assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, what is P(τ_a<∞)?
  B: 1. The proof takes the limit of P(τ_a≤ n)=2(1-Φ(a/√n)) over integers n, which tends to 1. It is a statement about probability, not about each outcome.
SC1-c165 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Corollary 1.5, Section 1, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Corollary 1.5. Under the hypotheses of Proposition 1.2, and assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, what density does the law of τ_a have on (0,∞)?
  B: f_a(H)=a H^(-3/2)e^(-a^2/(2H))/√(2π) for H>0. It is obtained by differentiating F(H)=2(1-Φ(a H^(-1/2))).
SC1-c166 [L08] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Remark 1.6. Does Lesson 8 compute or bound the expectation E[τ_a]?
  B: No. It gives the probability of finiteness and the law, and it states no value or bound for the mean. The truncated means of Lab 1, Part B are means of the walk's first-passage index ν_1 (Definition 2.1 of this lesson) capped at N, not of τ_a.
SC1-c167 [L08] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Definition 2.1. For an integer a≥ 1, what is the first-passage index ν_a of the simple random walk?
  B: ν_a= min{k≥ 1: S_k≥ a}, with ν_a=∞ if no such k exists.
SC1-c168 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Proposition 2.2, Section 2, with Corollary 1.4 of Lesson 6
  F: Proposition 2.2. For integers a≥ 1 and N≥ 1, how are the events {ν_a≤ N} and { max_(k≤ N)S_k≥ a} related, and what is the count of {ν_a≤ N}?
  B: They coincide on every step sequence. The count is #{S_N≥ a}+#{S_N>a} over the 2^N sequences.
SC1-c169 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Proof of Proposition 2.3, Section 2
  F: Proposition 2.3. For integers a≥ 1 and N≥ 1, what is E[ min(ν_a,N)] for the simple random walk?
  B: Σ_(k=0)^(N-1)P(ν_a>k), where P(ν_a>k)=1-P( max_(0≤ j≤ k)S_j≥ a).
SC1-c170 [L08] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part A (exact enumeration of 2^n sequences)
  F: Lab 1, Part A (exact enumeration): on how many (sequence, level) pairs, with n ≤ 12 and a ≤ n, does the first-passage event agree with the maximum event?
  B: All 90114 (sequence, level) pairs: for each of the 78 pairs (n, a) with n ≤ 12 and a ≤ n, all 2^n step sequences. The counts also agree with the walk identity on all 78 pairs.
SC1-c171 [L08] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part A (exact enumeration)
  F: Lab 1, Part A: for n = 12 and a = 3, what are the first-passage counts at steps 3 to 12, and what are P(ν_3 ≤ 12) and P(S_12 ≥ 3)?
  B: 512, 0, 384, 0, 288, 0, 224, 0, 180, 0. They sum to 1588 of 4096, so P(ν_3 ≤ 12) = 0.387695. P(S_12 ≥ 3) = 794/4096 = 0.193848.
SC1-c172 [L08] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part B (exact fractions, sum of P(ν_1 > k))
  F: Lab 1, Part B: what are the exact truncated means E[min(ν_1, N)] for N = 16, 64 and 256?
  B: 5.480560, 11.815731 and 24.557203. They increase with N. They are capped means of the walk's first-passage index ν_1, not the Brownian mean E[τ_1].
SC1-c173 [L08] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part C (SIMULATED, one seed)
  F: Lab 1, Part C (SIMULATED, one seed, 1000 walks of length 256): how do the sample means of min(ν_1, N) compare with the exact values, and with what z values (z = (sample mean − exact)/standard error)?
  B: Sample means 5.2850, 11.3840 and 23.1240 against exact 5.4806, 11.8157 and 24.5572, with z = −1.05, −0.69 and −0.77. All three lie below the exact values by less than 1.1 standard errors.
SC1-c174 [L08] grade=C cite=[] basis=Lab 1, Part D (numerical evaluation of the closed forms of Corollaries 1.3 and 1.5, which assume A3 and A4 of Lesson 5)
  F: Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, Lab 1, Part D: for a = 1, what are P(τ_1 ≤ H) and the density of τ_1 at H = 1 and H = 2?
  B: P(τ_1 ≤ 1) = 0.317311 and density 0.241971 at H = 1. P(τ_1 ≤ 2) = 0.479500 and density 0.109848 at H = 2. Each density matches a central difference of the distribution function to 1e-8.
SC1-c175 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Section 3 (Where the canon breaks), direct example
  F: Section 3: can {τ_a≤ H}={M_H≥ a} fail for a path with a jump? Give the example.
  B: Yes. A path that is 0 on [0, 1/2), jumps to 2 at 1/2, and stays at 2 has M_1 = 2 ≥ 1 but never equals 1, so τ_1 = ∞ and {τ_1 ≤ 1} fails.
SC1-c176 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Corollary 1.4 (assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5) and Section 4
  F: Misconception: τ_a is finite on every path. What does Corollary 1.4 establish?
  B: Only that, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, P(τ_a < ∞) = 1. It concerns the probability of the event {τ_a = ∞}, which is zero, and does not say that this event is empty.
SC1-c178 [L08] grade=None cite=[] basis=None
  F: Misconception: finiteness with probability one implies that the mean of τ_a is finite. Is this right?
  B: No. Finiteness does not bound a mean. No moment of τ_a is evaluated, and no limit is stated for the truncated means.
SC1-c179 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem M3, Proposition 2.2
  F: Problem M3. For the simple random walk of 3 steps, what is P(ν_1 ≤ 3)?
  B: 5/8. Four sequences start with +1, and one more, (−1, +1, +1), reaches 1. The walk identity gives #{S_3 ≥ 1} + #{S_3 > 1} = 4 + 1 = 5.
SC1-c180 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem E1, parity of the walk
  F: Problem E1. For the walk with a = 3 and n = 12, why are the first-passage counts zero at even steps?
  B: S_k has the parity of k, so S_k = 3 needs k odd. The odd counts 512, 384, 288, 224 and 180 sum to 1588.
SC1-c181 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem R1, Proposition 2.2
  F: Problem R1. Show that #{ν_a ≤ N} = 2 #{S_N ≥ a} − #{S_N = a}. For N = 12 and a = 3, what does this give?
  B: The identity follows from Proposition 2.2. Since S_12 is even, #{S_12 = 3} = 0, so #{ν_3 ≤ 12} = 2 × 794 = 1588.
SC1-c182 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem R2, Proposition 2.3
  F: Problem R2. Show that E[min(ν_a, N+1)] − E[min(ν_a, N)] = P(ν_a > N). What follows about the truncated mean?
  B: The difference is the expectation of the indicator 1{ν_a > N}, which is nonnegative. So the truncated mean is nondecreasing in N.
SC1-c183 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Problem R3, Corollary 1.5, assuming A3 and A4
  F: Problem R3. For fixed a > 0, where is the density f_a of τ_a largest, assuming A3 and A4?
  B: At H = a²/3. The log-derivative is (a² − 3H)/(2H²), which is positive before a²/3 and negative after.
SC1-c191 [L07] grade=T cite=[] basis=Corollary 1.5(a) with m=0, Remark 1.4 (Φ(0)=1/2) and Theorem 1.3, assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5
  F: Misconception: the running maximum has the same law as B_H. Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, what are P(M_H≤ 0) and P(B_H≤ 0) for H>0?
  B: P(M_H≤ 0)=2Φ(0)−1=0, by Corollary 1.5(a) with m=0. P(B_H≤ 0)=Φ(0)=1/2. So the two laws differ at 0.
SC1-c201 [L08] grade=T cite=[] basis=Corollary 1.3 (assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5) and Lab 1, Part D
  F: Misconception: the first passage to a has the same probability as the terminal value, P(τ_a ≤ H) = P(B_H ≥ a). Assuming A3 and A4 of Lesson 5, is this right?
  B: No. Corollary 1.3 gives P(τ_a ≤ H) = 2 P(B_H ≥ a) = 2(1−Φ(a/√H)) = erfc(a/√(2H)) for a > 0 and H > 0, under A3 and A4. At H = 1 and a = 1, P(τ_1 ≤ 1) = 0.317311 and P(B_1 ≥ 1) = 0.1586552539 (Lab 1, Part D; Lesson 7, Part D).
